: The kitchen-sink thread: thinking, a tool group with chart + table artifacts,
: a sub-agent (scoped frames), an artifact reference, a long pause, and two
: turns that end by asking the user a question — one question the first time,
: three the second, one of them multi-select. Both have their follow-up queued.

event: final
data: {"type": "user", "id": "u1", "content": "How did the Q3 launch campaigns do?", "timestamp": "2026-08-03T10:21:44Z"}

event: final
data: {"type": "assistant", "id": "a1", "content": "Overall Q3 launch spend was €48.2k across three channels with a blended ROAS of 3.1. Want the breakdown?"}

: turn

event: final
data: {"type": "thinking", "id": "t2", "content": "The user wants the Q3 breakdown. Before I pull anything I should decide what \"how did they do\" actually means here, because the honest answer depends on which frame we use.\n\nThe obvious read is blended ROAS by channel. That's what most people expect and it's cheap to produce, but it's also the read most likely to mislead. Q3 spans the mid-August attribution window change, so reported conversions before and after aren't strictly comparable. If I present a simple channel table someone will draw a conclusion about LinkedIn that the data doesn't support.\n\nSo: pull daily spend and conversions grouped by channel and day rather than a single aggregate. Day-level lets me see whether any shift lines up with the window change rather than with an actual performance change. I want the trend visible, not just the totals — a chart of weekly spend per channel will make the mid-August LinkedIn peak obvious in a way a table won't.\n\nThe thing I actually suspect: LinkedIn spend climbed through August while conversions flattened. There are three plausible causes. Creative fatigue, which would show as declining CTR over the same window. Audience saturation, which would show as rising frequency. Or self-competition — two campaigns bidding on overlapping segments, which inflates CPC without any change in demand. The launch used two segments that sound suspiciously adjacent, founders and marketing leads, and those populations overlap heavily on LinkedIn.\n\nI can't settle that from the spend data alone; it needs the audience specialist to compute actual member overlap between the two segment definitions. That's worth delegating rather than guessing, because the recommendation is completely different depending on the answer. If it's overlap, the fix is merging segments and costs nothing. If it's fatigue, the fix is new creative and costs a production cycle.\n\nOrder of operations: spend query first so I have the shape, then the overlap check in parallel since it doesn't depend on the spend numbers, then synthesize. I should present Google Ads honestly too — it's unglamorous but it's carrying the account, and the temptation is to spend the whole answer on the channel that looks broken."}

event: final
